Alright, no formalities. Let's focus on studies.

81
00:03:48,110 --> 00:03:51,264
So, the first Codex capability to understand

82
00:03:51,264 --> 00:03:53,098
is local file operations,

83
00:03:53,098 --> 00:03:54,811
its defining capability,

84
00:03:54,811 --> 00:03:57,940
setting it apart from conversational AI's.

85
00:03:58,140 --> 00:04:00,425
Because in the past, when we used ChatGPT,

86
00:04:00,425 --> 00:04:02,746
if we wanted it to see our local content,

87
00:04:02,746 --> 00:04:04,140
we only had two options.

88
00:04:04,460 --> 00:04:07,167
One way is to copy and paste it into the chat,

89
00:04:07,167 --> 00:04:09,359
and the other is to upload the files

90
00:04:09,359 --> 00:04:12,040
within the formats and quantities it allows.

91
00:04:12,280 --> 00:04:13,859
But Codex is different.

92
00:04:13,859 --> 00:04:17,704
It can independently read and manipulate our local files,

93
00:04:17,704 --> 00:04:19,559
with no limits on quantity.

94
00:04:19,769 --> 00:04:21,541
For example, as an editor,

95
00:04:21,541 --> 00:04:24,915
I have over eighty video clips stored locally

96
00:04:24,915 --> 00:04:26,790
that I need for my edits.

97
00:04:27,110 --> 00:04:30,310
But the filenames are all a mess— totally incomprehensible.

98
00:04:30,520 --> 00:04:33,432
So, I want to rename them based on their content,

99
00:04:33,432 --> 00:04:35,676
so that when we look at the filenames,

100
00:04:35,676 --> 00:04:37,800
we'll know what each clip is about.

101
00:04:37,920 --> 00:04:40,893
Now, ChatGPT definitely can't do this,

102
00:04:40,893 --> 00:04:43,866
but Codex can. Notice these two places;

103
00:04:43,866 --> 00:04:46,439
both display a concept: 'Project'.

104
00:04:46,680 --> 00:04:48,003
So let's select that,

105
00:04:48,003 --> 00:04:49,751
and once we choose 'Project,'

106
00:04:49,751 --> 00:04:52,240
it prompts us to pick a local folder.

107
00:04:52,560 --> 00:04:57,580
So, once we select this folder, it becomes our project.

108
00:04:57,940 --> 00:05:01,172
Codex can freely read and manipulate all the files inside.

109
00:05:01,172 --> 00:05:03,959
Of course, this requires us to grant it permissions.

110
00:05:04,280 --> 00:05:07,037
Look at the permission options here.

111
00:05:07,037 --> 00:05:09,164
We're currently on default,

112
00:05:09,164 --> 00:05:12,000
but clicking it reveals three modes.

113
00:05:12,250 --> 00:05:14,612
So, the default permissions mean that

114
00:05:14,612 --> 00:05:16,318
within our current folder,

115
00:05:16,318 --> 00:05:19,009
it can freely read and write these files.

116
00:05:19,250 --> 00:05:22,183
But if it wants to connect to the internet,

117
00:05:22,183 --> 00:05:23,949
download something for us,

118
00:05:23,949 --> 00:05:26,918
or touch any files outside of this folder,

119
00:05:26,918 --> 00:05:28,120
it can't do that.

120
00:05:28,440 --> 00:05:29,647
Should it need to,

121
00:05:29,647 --> 00:05:32,274
it will ask for elevated permissions,

122
00:05:32,274 --> 00:05:34,760
requiring our agreement to proceed.

123
00:05:35,060 --> 00:05:37,058
Auto Review, as the name implies,

124
00:05:37,058 --> 00:05:39,766
means an AI automatically reviews actions,

125
00:05:39,766 --> 00:05:41,700
deciding if they're dangerous.

126
00:05:41,940 --> 00:05:44,453
If it's risky, it'll ask us; otherwise,

127
00:05:44,453 --> 00:05:47,644
for safe tasks like official software downloads,

128
00:05:47,644 --> 00:05:48,799
it'll just do it.

129
00:05:48,960 --> 00:05:52,552
Auto review is more flexible and smarter than default,

130
00:05:52,552 --> 00:05:54,180
so just choose this one.

131
00:05:54,380 --> 00:05:56,460
And finally, there's Full Access.

132
00:05:56,730 --> 00:05:59,703
This means Codex can do pretty much anything

133
00:05:59,703 --> 00:06:03,082
on your computer—it can modify or delete any file,

134
00:06:03,082 --> 00:06:04,840
and run commands anywhere.

135
00:06:05,120 --> 00:06:08,670
For example, it might delete your Claude Code.

136
00:06:08,670 --> 00:06:11,668
Kidding aside, it usually won't act up.

137
00:06:11,668 --> 00:06:13,088
AI has no jealousy.

138
00:06:13,890 --> 00:06:13,890
Oh.

139
00:06:15,040 --> 00:06:17,464
So for everyone, especially beginners,

140
00:06:17,464 --> 00:06:20,158
just pick auto review and trust the AI.

141
00:06:20,158 --> 00:06:22,919
Can we really review better than it, right?

142
00:06:23,240 --> 00:06:26,007
OK, so we've selected that video folder from earlier,

143
00:06:26,007 --> 00:06:28,720
and let's go on and make our unreasonable demands.

144
00:06:28,980 --> 00:06:32,833
Have it rename all the video files in this folder

145
00:06:32,833 --> 00:06:35,980
based on what's actually in the footage.

146
00:06:36,520 --> 00:06:37,680
It's starting.

147
00:06:38,280 --> 00:06:41,349
And look, it's being super smart—first, it checked

148
00:06:41,349 --> 00:06:43,460
how many videos we actually have.

149
00:06:43,780 --> 00:06:46,565
Then, it chose to extract key frames

150
00:06:46,565 --> 00:06:49,350
to figure out what the content was.

151
00:06:49,510 --> 00:06:52,296
Plus, it even knew not to look at each image one by one;

152
00:06:52,296 --> 00:06:54,778
instead, it stitched them together into thumbnails

153
00:06:54,778 --> 00:06:56,349
and viewed them group by group.

154
00:06:57,270 --> 00:06:58,302
OK, and just like that,

155
00:06:58,302 --> 00:07:00,810
all the filenames in this folder have been changed.

156
00:07:01,060 --> 00:07:04,053
And Codex didn't ask for elevated permissions,

157
00:07:04,053 --> 00:07:06,980
as all operations stayed within this folder.

158
00:07:07,150 --> 00:07:09,995
So, let's check if everything lines up correctly,

159
00:07:09,995 --> 00:07:10,469
shall we?

160
00:07:10,950 --> 00:07:13,455
Okay, pianist concert hall, right.

161
00:07:13,455 --> 00:07:15,665
Snowy mountain combat, right.

162
00:07:15,665 --> 00:07:19,760
Cheongsam woman in Night Alley selling hot drinks.

163
00:07:20,160 --> 00:07:26,010
Wow, and the naming even follows a clear format: number,

164
00:07:26,010 --> 00:07:28,550
character, scene, action.

165
00:07:28,910 --> 00:07:30,780
The level of detail AI puts in ... man,

166
00:07:30,780 --> 00:07:32,430
you just gotta give it credit.

167
00:07:32,640 --> 00:07:36,165
Also, we can actually start multiple different conversations

168
00:07:36,165 --> 00:07:37,600
within the same project.

169
00:07:37,840 --> 00:07:40,022
Their contexts are all different,

170
00:07:40,022 --> 00:07:42,309
but they can all access our files

171
00:07:42,309 --> 00:07:44,320
and this project at any time.

172
00:07:44,560 --> 00:07:46,781
For example, in another conversation,

173
00:07:46,781 --> 00:07:48,876
we can give it a different task:

174
00:07:48,876 --> 00:07:52,366
have it figure out how to edit all the landscape videos

175
00:07:52,366 --> 00:07:53,699
into one single clip.

176
00:07:54,990 --> 00:07:56,990
Wow, it can actually edit!

177
00:07:57,310 --> 00:07:59,429
It figured out some tools on its own,

178
00:07:59,429 --> 00:08:01,158
asked us for some permissions,

179
00:08:01,158 --> 00:08:04,080
and then used FFmpeg to stitch them all together.

180
00:08:04,640 --> 00:08:08,000
It's all the landscape shots, edited into one clip.

181
00:08:08,570 --> 00:08:11,278
Okay, so if we want to create a new project,

182
00:08:11,278 --> 00:08:13,664
we can manually create it right here,

183
00:08:13,664 --> 00:08:16,675
and it will also automatically create a folder

184
00:08:16,675 --> 00:08:18,050
on our local machine.

185
00:08:18,380 --> 00:08:21,534
And throughout our conversation with Codex,

186
00:08:21,534 --> 00:08:24,873
any files the Agent generates—PDF's, Markdown,

187
00:08:24,873 --> 00:08:28,820
PPTs—will be saved directly onto our local computer.

188
00:08:29,220 --> 00:08:31,298
Okay, so by now you should get it.

189
00:08:31,298 --> 00:08:35,077
Codex can access our local files at any time to read content,

190
00:08:35,077 --> 00:08:35,832
write files,

191
00:08:35,832 --> 00:08:38,038
delete files, and move files around.

192
00:08:38,260 --> 00:08:41,860
The folder's contents become the context

193
00:08:41,860 --> 00:08:44,200
Codex can access anytime.

194
00:08:44,200 --> 00:08:48,700
So, each 'project' here corresponds to a local folder.

195
00:08:49,020 --> 00:08:53,460
So, how much context would a task like that just now cost us?

196
00:08:54,540 --> 00:08:56,740
We can see it right here.

197
00:08:56,900 --> 00:08:58,930
So, this circle indicates

198
00:08:58,930 --> 00:09:02,062
how much GPT five point five context

199
00:09:02,062 --> 00:09:06,040
this conversation and its operations have used.

200
00:09:06,290 --> 00:09:09,425
Hovering over it shows more details.

201
00:09:09,425 --> 00:09:14,650
As context fills up, Codex automatically compresses it for us.

202
00:09:14,690 --> 00:09:16,744
But actually, once a task is wrapped up,

203
00:09:16,744 --> 00:09:19,374
you don't have to wait for it to get almost full

204
00:09:19,374 --> 00:09:20,690
for it to auto compress.

205
00:09:20,890 --> 00:09:24,932
We can also manually compress the context ourselves

206
00:09:24,932 --> 00:09:27,310
by using the '/compress' command.

207
00:09:27,550 --> 00:09:30,242
This helps the model focus better on our new task

208
00:09:30,242 --> 00:09:32,550
and also saves a bit of our context quota.

209
00:09:32,750 --> 00:09:36,550
Of course, aside from this conversation context allowance,

210
00:09:36,550 --> 00:09:40,470
you're probably also wondering—whether you're a free user

211
00:09:40,470 --> 00:09:41,640
or a Plus member—

212
00:09:41,840 --> 00:09:44,920
how much total quota I have left to use, right?

213
00:09:46,190 --> 00:09:47,950
There are two ways to check this.

214
00:09:48,210 --> 00:09:49,733
First, on the left side,

215
00:09:49,733 --> 00:09:52,321
under Settings, click 'Remaining Quota'

216
00:09:52,321 --> 00:09:56,450
to see what's left for the next five hours and for the week.

217
00:09:56,680 --> 00:09:59,205
It even prompts when the quota resets,

218
00:09:59,205 --> 00:10:02,960
making its design far more user friendly than Claude's.

219
00:10:03,160 --> 00:10:04,172
Or, another way

220
00:10:04,172 --> 00:10:07,644
is to just type a forward slash in the chat box

221
00:10:07,644 --> 00:10:09,670
and find this 'status' option.

222
00:10:10,030 --> 00:10:11,833
So, once you open this status view,

223
00:10:11,833 --> 00:10:13,931
your session will continuously display

224
00:10:13,931 --> 00:10:17,190
your context allowance—those five hour and one week limits.

225
00:10:17,410 --> 00:10:20,601
Honestly, Codex's allowance is quite generous;

226
00:10:20,601 --> 00:10:22,374
its twenty dollars credit

227
00:10:22,374 --> 00:10:26,559
should easily cover most white collar users' frequent needs.

228
00:10:26,720 --> 00:10:27,693
OK, and while we're at it,

229
00:10:27,693 --> 00:10:29,600
let's also talk about the model selection here.

230
00:10:29,930 --> 00:10:31,430
So, once you click on that,

231
00:10:31,430 --> 00:10:34,610
we can select the speed. 'Fast' is like an express lane,

232
00:10:34,610 --> 00:10:36,770
which will use up more of our quota.

233
00:10:37,110 --> 00:10:38,571
You can pick other models here,

234
00:10:38,571 --> 00:10:41,236
but pretty much everyone goes with GPT five point five

235
00:10:41,236 --> 00:10:42,470
because it's just better.

236
00:10:42,810 --> 00:10:45,901
Then, select the intelligence level.

237
00:10:45,901 --> 00:10:48,903
Actually, Medium's usually enough,

238
00:10:48,903 --> 00:10:50,449
so we'll go High.

239
00:10:51,240 --> 00:10:53,191
OK, so this little microphone icon

240
00:10:53,191 --> 00:10:54,907
is obviously for voice input,

241
00:10:54,907 --> 00:10:56,800
which is way faster than typing.

242
00:10:56,980 --> 00:10:59,369
However, its transcription isn't as fast

243
00:10:59,369 --> 00:11:00,963
as a dedicated voice app.

244
00:11:00,963 --> 00:11:03,659
Still, I'd highly recommend using voice input.

245
00:11:03,780 --> 00:11:07,116
Alright, so through that local file processing example,

246
00:11:07,116 --> 00:11:10,830
we've now gotten a look at some of Codex's common features.

247
00:11:11,110 --> 00:11:14,790
Codex's second capability is command line tools.

248
00:11:15,100 --> 00:11:18,304
Regarding permissions, we noted that by default,

249
00:11:18,304 --> 00:11:20,255
it can read and write files,

250
00:11:20,255 --> 00:11:23,530
but not execute commands or network operations.

251
00:11:23,810 --> 00:11:25,610
So, what do these commands mean?

252
00:11:25,850 --> 00:11:29,460
The previous example used it to extract video frames

253
00:11:29,460 --> 00:11:31,960
and stitch multiple videos together.

254
00:11:32,160 --> 00:11:35,360
The FFmpeg it used is a command line tool.

255
00:11:35,500 --> 00:11:37,383
And there are some familiar commands,

256
00:11:37,383 --> 00:11:40,260
like when we clone a GitHub repository in the terminal.

257
00:11:40,420 --> 00:11:43,264
Or, as you're likely familiar with, entering commands

258
00:11:43,264 --> 00:11:44,380
in the command line

259
00:11:44,380 --> 00:11:46,500
to download Node and its dependencies.

260
00:11:46,780 --> 00:11:49,208
So, Codex's second major capability

261
00:11:49,208 --> 00:11:51,208
is that, with our permission,

262
00:11:51,208 --> 00:11:54,780
it can use our terminal to do these things for us.

