426
00:19:22,020 --> 00:19:23,946
So, what about Auto Memory?

427
00:19:23,946 --> 00:19:26,983
Well, as we just saw under 'Personalization,'

428
00:19:26,983 --> 00:19:30,909
we need to manually turn on this Auto Memory feature.

429
00:19:31,190 --> 00:19:34,657
So, its mechanism is that once enabled, Codex will,

430
00:19:34,657 --> 00:19:37,114
after a conversation or task ends,

431
00:19:37,114 --> 00:19:39,860
or after a brief period of inactivity,

432
00:19:40,080 --> 00:19:42,600
It summarizes the conversation as a memory

433
00:19:42,600 --> 00:19:44,040
and stores it somewhere.

434
00:19:44,280 --> 00:19:45,440
But there are some rules:

435
00:19:45,690 --> 00:19:48,397
It won't remember short conversations,

436
00:19:48,397 --> 00:19:51,690
and summarizing them also consumes our quota.

437
00:19:51,930 --> 00:19:54,890
So, when our quota gets too low, it won't remember.

438
00:19:55,160 --> 00:19:59,035
OpenAI officially advises against manually editing

439
00:19:59,035 --> 00:20:00,741
this auto memory file;

440
00:20:00,741 --> 00:20:03,300
only delete unneeded information.

441
00:20:03,580 --> 00:20:05,579
So, this file looks something like this.

442
00:20:05,780 --> 00:20:08,048
It logs tasks individually,

443
00:20:08,048 --> 00:20:11,233
primarily noting the memory's source

444
00:20:11,233 --> 00:20:14,330
conversation and working directory.

445
00:20:14,610 --> 00:20:17,726
And later on, when certain keywords are triggered,

446
00:20:17,726 --> 00:20:19,544
it will recall this memory.

447
00:20:19,544 --> 00:20:20,647
Then, further down,

448
00:20:20,647 --> 00:20:23,829
are the user's preferences regarding this matter.

449
00:20:23,990 --> 00:20:26,358
Then, the fifth section contains reusable knowledge

450
00:20:26,358 --> 00:20:27,590
