00:35:23,860 --> 00:35:27,818
or say 'generate a post recommending Trending projects.'

783
00:35:27,818 --> 00:35:31,919
Codex will also automatically invoke it with such phrases.

784
00:35:32,080 --> 00:35:35,083
But since the real time Trending projects on GitHub

785
00:35:35,083 --> 00:35:36,320
haven't been updated,

786
00:35:36,480 --> 00:35:37,530
So if we call it again,

787
00:35:37,530 --> 00:35:39,726
it'll most likely generate a similar article,

788
00:35:39,726 --> 00:35:40,919
so we'll skip trying that.

789
00:35:41,200 --> 00:35:43,512
But I think you all get the idea

790
00:35:43,512 --> 00:35:47,125
now—Skills let us lock in a whole set of verified,

791
00:35:47,125 --> 00:35:49,510
well tuned workflows and methods.

792
00:35:49,720 --> 00:35:54,688
Next time, Codex can consistently output based on this.

793
00:35:54,688 --> 00:35:57,080
The next capability is MCP.

794
00:35:57,420 --> 00:35:59,392
MCP isn't widely used now,

795
00:35:59,392 --> 00:36:01,917
so I'll just give a quick demo.

796
00:36:01,917 --> 00:36:06,099
You'll find Codex's MCP under 'MCP Servers' in Settings.

797
00:36:06,500 --> 00:36:08,023
But for beginners looking at this,

798
00:36:08,023 --> 00:36:10,100
you'll probably have no idea what to fill in.

799
00:36:10,310 --> 00:36:12,432
So, as we mentioned earlier,

800
00:36:12,432 --> 00:36:16,813
the easiest way is to just copy the MCP link you want

801
00:36:16,813 --> 00:36:18,880
and let Codex install it.

802
00:36:19,000 --> 00:36:22,271
For example, let's give it a NotebookLM MCP.

803
00:36:22,271 --> 00:36:25,769
It'll guide us through authorization and login,

804
00:36:25,769 --> 00:36:27,519
and then we're all set.

805
00:36:27,830 --> 00:36:29,260
Let's try another.

806
00:36:29,260 --> 00:36:32,676
I have a notebook full of interview videos;

807
00:36:32,676 --> 00:36:33,629
let's ask it.

808
00:36:34,070 --> 00:36:35,309
So just like that,

809
00:36:35,309 --> 00:36:39,740
Codex can now directly access these external knowledge bases

810
00:36:39,740 --> 00:36:41,070
on our NotebookLM.

811
00:36:41,470 --> 00:36:45,540
OK, the last Codex capability is timed tasks.

812
00:36:45,810 --> 00:36:47,572
Actually, scheduled tasks themselves

813
00:36:47,572 --> 00:36:49,133
aren't anything special—things

814
00:36:49,133 --> 00:36:50,846
like reminding me to take my meds

815
00:36:50,846 --> 00:36:52,307
or drink water every morning,

816
00:36:52,307 --> 00:36:53,970
regular software can do that too.

817
00:36:54,150 --> 00:36:56,173
But we're saving it for last

818
00:36:56,173 --> 00:37:00,413
because it can combine all the skills we've learned before

819
00:37:00,413 --> 00:37:02,460
into a whole suite of tasks.

820
00:37:02,620 --> 00:37:06,865
The agent's automated, intelligent, and customized execution –

821
00:37:06,865 --> 00:37:08,879
that's the real beauty of it.

822
00:37:09,280 --> 00:37:12,091
For example, for the Trending project posts

823
00:37:12,091 --> 00:37:13,363
Skill we just made,

824
00:37:13,363 --> 00:37:16,892
we can set it to generate every three days or weekly,

825
00:37:16,892 --> 00:37:18,250
on a fixed schedule.

826
00:37:18,250 --> 00:37:20,970
Publish GitHub Trending project recommendations.

827
00:37:21,210 --> 00:37:24,255
Isn't that an automated social media manager?

828
00:37:24,255 --> 00:37:26,124
To create automated tasks,

829
00:37:26,124 --> 00:37:27,369
there are two ways.

830
00:37:27,560 --> 00:37:30,059
The first way is to select the Automation panel

831
00:37:30,059 --> 00:37:32,879
over here. It provides some official sample templates,

832
00:37:32,879 --> 00:37:35,220
though most of them are development related.

833
00:37:35,420 --> 00:37:37,703
So, let's create a new automation.

834
00:37:37,703 --> 00:37:38,671
Filling it out

835
00:37:38,671 --> 00:37:42,823
is quite straightforward—you simply use prompts to describe

836
00:37:42,823 --> 00:37:44,069
what it should do.

837
00:37:44,350 --> 00:37:47,003
Then, we can choose when it should trigger here,

838
00:37:47,003 --> 00:37:49,939
and we can also customize the trigger time ourselves.

839
00:37:50,380 --> 00:37:52,647
Then, you can select the execution model

840
00:37:52,647 --> 00:37:54,275
and thinking strength here,

841
00:37:54,275 --> 00:37:56,019
and finally, just click 'create'.

842
00:37:56,190 --> 00:37:59,625
But still, I'd recommend the second, simpler method:

843
00:37:59,625 --> 00:38:02,430
just telling Codex directly in the chat.

844
00:38:02,710 --> 00:38:06,170
So let's just tell it, "Help me create an automated task."

845
00:38:06,490 --> 00:38:07,700
Every Monday at nine AM,

846
00:38:07,700 --> 00:38:10,886
automatically run the 'Trending Project Recommendations' Skill,

847
00:38:10,886 --> 00:38:13,330
generate a post, and send it to the group chat.

848
00:38:13,930 --> 00:38:16,361
OK, so if we check back under 'Automation,'

849
00:38:16,361 --> 00:38:18,668
it's already set up this task for us,

850
00:38:18,668 --> 00:38:20,850
and we can change its time anytime.

851
00:38:21,320 --> 00:38:23,620
Or, let's say your email, plugins,

852
00:38:23,620 --> 00:38:26,960
GitHub, and Feishu are all connected to Codex.

853
00:38:27,200 --> 00:38:30,344
Then you can have it summarize your completed calendar

854
00:38:30,344 --> 00:38:31,800
meetings daily at six PM.

855
00:38:32,000 --> 00:38:34,657
using my GitHub commits and email replies

856
00:38:34,657 --> 00:38:37,640
to generate a comprehensive daily work report.

857
00:38:38,920 --> 00:38:38,928
Lastly, for phone control, it's simple: just update your phone's ChatGPT and PC's Codex to their latest versions. Then ...

858
00:38:47,860 --> 00:38:50,145
Just open ChatGPT on your phone,

859
00:38:50,145 --> 00:38:51,931
tap Codex in the sidebar,

860
00:38:51,931 --> 00:38:53,438
and it'll prompt you

861
00:38:53,438 --> 00:38:57,960
to let this phone control your computer—done just like that.

862
00:38:58,240 --> 00:38:59,701
And if you have multiple computers,

863
00:38:59,701 --> 00:39:01,120
you can connect those up as well.

864
00:39:01,240 --> 00:39:03,158
So that means whether you're out on the street,

865
00:39:03,158 --> 00:39:05,160
on the subway, or at a get together with friends,

866
00:39:05,370 --> 00:39:08,553
you can assign a task anytime, anywhere from your phone

867
00:39:08,553 --> 00:39:11,325
and have Codex get a job done on your computer,

868
00:39:11,325 --> 00:39:13,482
making it feel more like a colleague

869
00:39:13,482 --> 00:39:15,100
you can work with remotely.

870
00:39:15,310 --> 00:39:18,858
This feature is currently available on the free plan,

871
00:39:18,858 --> 00:39:22,134
but it mainly connects to Codex on Mac systems.

872
00:39:22,134 --> 00:39:25,819
Okay, so that wraps up all of Codex's core capabilities.

873
00:39:26,020 --> 00:39:26,979
Let's recap.

874
00:39:27,280 --> 00:39:30,000
Codex can manipulate our local files.

875
00:39:30,230 --> 00:39:33,672
it can use the terminal command line tools on our computer,

876
00:39:33,672 --> 00:39:36,122
it has two ways to build persistent memory,

877
00:39:36,122 --> 00:39:37,989
and it can also generate images.

878
00:39:38,390 --> 00:39:41,157
Plus, it can handle entire project development

879
00:39:41,157 --> 00:39:42,510
through its Plan mode.

880
00:39:42,870 --> 00:39:44,644
Plus, it has rich plugins

881
00:39:44,644 --> 00:39:47,380
to control your browser and computer,

882
00:39:47,380 --> 00:39:51,670
and can turn knowledge and workflows into reusable Skills.

883
00:39:51,890 --> 00:39:54,793
It connects to external services via MCP

884
00:39:54,793 --> 00:39:56,680
and sets automated tasks.

885
00:39:56,680 --> 00:39:58,711
But more than these features,

886
00:39:58,711 --> 00:40:01,179
I want you to remember two things.

887
00:40:01,370 --> 00:40:03,481
First, there are so many things

888
00:40:03,481 --> 00:40:05,664
we can now hand over to Agents.

889
00:40:05,664 --> 00:40:09,936
And second, we've moved from the phase of asking AI questions

890
00:40:09,936 --> 00:40:11,930
to the phase of managing AI.

891
00:40:12,160 --> 00:40:14,135
Because in the past, when we used ChatGPT,

892
00:40:14,135 --> 00:40:15,172
we'd have a question,

893
00:40:15,172 --> 00:40:16,209
ask it, get an answer,

894
00:40:16,209 --> 00:40:17,000
and that was it.

895
00:40:17,260 --> 00:40:21,320
Now with agents like Codex, we must act as leaders:

896
00:40:21,320 --> 00:40:24,430
preparing its context and environment,

897
00:40:24,430 --> 00:40:27,120
and setting its task objectives.

898
00:40:27,380 --> 00:40:28,689
then check its plan,

899
00:40:28,689 --> 00:40:31,860
supervise its process, and approve its results.

900
00:40:32,100 --> 00:40:34,719
The leader is also responsible for

901
00:40:34,719 --> 00:40:39,034
turning good methods and workflows into reusable skills

902
00:40:39,034 --> 00:40:41,500
and automating repetitive tasks.

903
00:40:42,510 --> 00:40:45,138
Alright! Congrats on all your promotions today!

904
00:40:45,138 --> 00:40:48,350
Remember to like, favorite, and follow—see you next time!

